    "title": "pandas.TimedeltaIndex.days — pandas 3.0.5 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.TimedeltaIndex.days#\n\n- property TimedeltaIndex.days[source]#\n- Number of days for each element. See also \n  - `Series.dt.seconds`\n  - Return number of seconds for each element.\n  - `Series.dt.microseconds`\n  - Return number of microseconds for each element.\n  - `Series.dt.nanoseconds`\n  - Return number of nanoseconds for each element.\n Examples For Series: >>> ser = pd.Series(pd.to_timedelta([1, 2, 3], unit='D')) >>> ser 0 1 days 1 2 days 2 3 days dtype: timedelta64[s] >>> ser.dt.days 0 1 1 2 2 3 dtype: int64 For TimedeltaIndex: >>> tdelta_idx = pd.to_timedelta([\"0 days\", \"10 days\", \"20 days\"]) >>> tdelta_idx TimedeltaIndex(['0 days', '10 days', '20 days'], dtype='timedelta64[us]', freq=None) >>> tdelta_idx.days Index([0, 10, 20], dtype='int64')",
    "markdown_length": 765,
